<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of San Francisco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as San Francisco Peace Treaty, Treaty of Peace with Japan · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> was signed on 8 September 1951 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and 48 Allied nations, formally ending the state of war of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It took effect on 28 April 1952, ending the Allied occupation and restoring Japan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and Japan gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and other territories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> did not sign, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was not invited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco.jpg" alt="Treaty of San Francisco" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshida_signs_San_Francisco_Peace_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty, also called the Treaty of Peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, was signed on 8 September 1951 at the War Memorial Opera House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. China was not invited because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom disagreed on whether the Republic of China on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> represented the Chinese people; the United States recognised the former, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had recognised the latter in 1950. As a compromise neither was invited. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was not invited for a similar disagreement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Portugal were not invited either. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> were invited but did not take part. India considered some provisions to limit Japanese sovereignty, and signed a separate treaty on 9 June 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty ended the legal state of war, brought the Allied occupation to an end and returned full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It came into force on 28 April 1952. Japan renounced its rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, Formosa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spratly-islands">Spratly Islands</a>, Antarctica and South Sakhalin. Article 3 left the Bonin, Volcano and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a> under a potential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trusteeship, which was never set up. In Article 11 Japan accepted the judgments of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East and other Allied war crimes courts. Article 14 recognised that Japan should pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> but that its resources were insufficient to make complete reparation, so it would negotiate with Allied states whose territories it had occupied.</p><p>Under Article 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> transferred assets to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-committee-of-the-red-cross">International Committee of the Red Cross</a> for former prisoners of war and paid £4,500,000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and South Vietnam received compensation in 1956 and 1959, and the last payment went to the Philippines on 22 July 1976. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> later signed bilateral treaties instead. The Soviet delegation under Andrei Gromyko opposed the draft and refused to sign, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Japan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ended the war by a Joint Declaration on 19 October 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Together with the Security Treaty signed the same day, the treaty began what is called the San Francisco System, which defines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It left some questions open. It did not name a recipient for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, which was absent, has never recognised the treaty. The absence of clarity over which islands Japan gave up gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a> dispute. A separate treaty between Japan and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> on 28 April 1952, hours before the San Francisco treaty took effect. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> did not sign, it was not entitled to the benefits of Article 14, and in 1965 Japan agreed to settlements with its government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of San Francisco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of San Francisco</b> came into force, ending the Allied occupation of Japan that had begun in 1945.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>San Francisco Peace Treaty</b>, signed in 1951, took effect on 28 April 1952, restoring Japan's sovereignty alongside a security treaty with the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-japan">Occupied Japan</a></li></ul></section>
